  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nHigh Rainfall Chances Sat Night/Sunday: The main trough will\nmove into the area late Saturday night. A front will move into\nthe area from the northwest and will be the focus for what looks\nlike a widespread rain. The majority of short term guidance \nshows rain moving in after 10pm with the bulk of the rain after\nmidnight, lasting through the morning hours Sunday. Mean MU \nCAPE values still look to remain below 500 j/kg (perhaps \nstruggling to reach 250j/kg) therefore any thunderstorms will be\nisolated at best. \n\nLatest NBM guidance continues to highlight rainfall amounts\nbetween 0.10-0.30in across most of the area. The higher end of\nguidance does show a few locations receiving 0.50in, mainly\nnorthwest of Springfield. The lowest amounts are expected\nsoutheast of Springfield. The bulk of the rainfall looks to move\nout by Sunday afternoon with an increase north winds and cold\nair advection.\n\nCooler Next Week: Ensembles generally support a pattern of high\npressure across the northern US and Great Lakes for at least the\nfirst half of the week. This will lead to northerly winds and\ncolder/dry conditions. NBM temp spreads are small which leads to\nhigher confidence in high temps remaining below 50 degrees.\nEnsemble variance/uncertainty increases significantly after\nThursday with low confidence in the upper level pattern.\nRainfall chances remain below 10 percent currently for most of\nnext week.",
